I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern format, and the provided tungstate workflow/tooling first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, refine them in GSAS-II, and verify that every submitted artifact is generated from the final project.

The chemistry points to a Ca–W–O reaction with possible residual CaCO₃/WO₃, but I’m treating that only as a candidate set. I’m now extracting peaks and checking the frozen COD library plus the stated instrument/specimen ranges so the phase count is driven by the full profile.

The peak pattern already resolves three recognizable families: monoclinic WO₃ (the strong 23–25° triplet), scheelite-type CaWO₄, and calcite CaCO₃. I’m screening all relevant COD polymorphs now because the exact lattice/atomic model matters for the final replay, especially for WO₃.

The COD screen strongly favors COD 2311041 for the dominant monoclinic WO₃; its diagnostic triplet intensities match much better than the other WO₃ models. The secondary peak positions independently match scheelite CaWO₄ (COD 7219229) and room-temperature calcite (COD 1547347). I’ll now refine those three together and use residual peaks to test whether a fourth crystalline phase is warranted.

The three-phase refinement is behaving consistently: the first quantitative estimate is about 57 wt% WO₃, 36 wt% CaCO₃, and 7 wt% CaWO₄. The remaining misfit is mostly peak shape, not missing reflections. I found the unconstrained profile fit was trading instrument broadening against crystallite broadening, so I’m imposing physically valid bounds and refining phase size/strain before the shared instrument profile.

The bounded refinement is running through repeated convergence passes. Peak positions and phase scales are stable; this pass is chiefly separating common instrumental width from phase-specific size/microstrain, which is the main source of fraction bias at this point.

The coordinate refinement is now adjusting the two independent W sites in WO₃ first, then the oxygen sublattices. This is the appropriate structural test here: if the phase assignment were wrong, these small symmetry-preserving shifts would not remove the systematic intensity residuals.

The isolated effect screens show no meaningful surface roughness—the fit drives both roughness parameters to zero. Texture and directional-strain screens are still evaluating the allowed crystallographic axes independently so they cannot borrow improvement from unrelated parameters.

The effect screens select one clear non-isotropic term: WO₃ microstrain along [010]. Calcite also benefits from weak [001] texture, while CaWO₄ does not. I’m integrating those with one shared Uiso per phase—the physically correct generator model—before touching coordinates again.

The refined three-phase fractions have settled near 55.5% WO₃, 33.4% CaCO₃, and 11.1% CaWO₄. No unexplained peak family remains. I’m now re-screening texture and directional broadening against the improved structural model; this separates genuine specimen effects from intensity errors that the first-pass coordinates had been absorbing.

The remaining gap is mostly peak-shape rather than phase identity or atomic intensities: a Le Bail diagnostic only lowers Rwp to about 6.4%. I’m addressing the strong width-parameter correlations with several physically bounded profile starts; this is the cleanest way to avoid a local minimum in the instrument/size/strain partition.

One alternate profile basin has converged, but it is not better than simply improving the smooth background representation. Several more distant starts are numerically singular; I’m keeping only solutions that actually lower the observed-profile objective and remain within the physical width bounds.

The phase assignment is now secure: monoclinic WO₃, scheelite CaWO₄, and residual calcite CaCO₃. I’m finishing the profile optimization, then I’ll export and cross-check the CIFs, fractions, profile, and GSAS-II project as one internally consistent result.

The randomized searches did not beat the physically constrained model; they mostly pushed width terms to bounds. I’m now doing a conservative final pass from the best model—retaining only changes that improve the fit while preserving the generator’s allowed parameter ranges.

The retained profile gives Rwp ≈ 7.05% and stable normalized fractions of about 55.7% WO₃, 11.5% CaWO₄, and 32.8% CaCO₃. The remaining trials worsened the residual or hit physical bounds, so I’m freezing the model and checking structural geometry and file-to-project consistency before submission.

All three exported structures pass basic composition, symmetry, density, and contact checks. The evaluator-style sandbox replay could not start because this environment disables unprivileged namespaces, so I’m running the same read-only GSAS forward-replay code directly in a temporary folder instead.

The independent forward calculation exposed a small GSAS cache mismatch: the saved profile array understated the true replayed Rwp by 0.026 percentage points, although the structural model and fractions were unchanged. I’m correcting `fit.csv`, the project’s cached curve, and the reported statistics to the independently regenerated 7.081% value so every submitted artifact agrees.

The final files now use the same independently replayed curve, and the GPX replay reports no negative-width, background, or polarization flags. I’m doing the last schema and cross-file checks now, including exact phase-fraction agreement between `result.json` and `final.gpx`.

Completed the three-phase Rietveld refinement:

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| Monoclinic WO₃ | 55.75 wt% |
| Scheelite CaWO₄ | 11.49 wt% |
| Calcite CaCO₃ | 32.76 wt% |

Final fit: Rwp 7.081%, Rp 5.109%, GOF 2.959. Fractions are normalized over crystalline phases; amorphous content cannot be quantified without an internal standard.

